Shoals.Curves.yield_curve_from_pillars: pillar times must be strictly increasing: index 2 has time 2.0, which does not exceed time 3.0 at index 1

`rate_at` reads the pillars through `Nautilus.Interpolation.linear_interp_sorted`,
which folds over the pairs tracking the previous time and takes the first
bracket straddling the query. Out of order it bracketed (1, 0.01) against
(3, 0.07) rather than (1, 0.01) against (2, 0.06): `rate_at(curve, 1.5)`
returned 0.025 where 0.035 is correct, with no trap, no NaN and no diagnostic.
The same unsorted pillars then propagate through `discount_factor` and
`discount_factor_with_basis` into every discounted price.
